  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nA weak ridge of high pressure will slowly drift east of the area\nthis afternoon into this evening. This will allow for light\nwinds this morning to slowly become south/southwest through the\nday. High/mid clouds early will depart this morning leaving \nmostly sunny skies with highs ranging from the mid 70s east to \nthe lower 80s west. Onshore winds will also keep areas near Lake\nMichigan cooler, mainly in the lower 70s.\n\nAn upper ridge will build across the area tonight into Monday\nallowing for high temps to reach the mid 80s areawide Monday\nwith some areas likely reaching the upper 80s. Southwest winds\nmay also gust into the 20-30 mph range Monday afternoon. Still \nsome uncertainty on the extent of cloud cover which may be a \nlimiting factor for temps/winds based on how much mixing occurs.\nStrong low level winds Monday night should keep low temps \nrather mild, perhaps in the upper 50s/lower 60s for much of the \narea. Consensus among the models now suggests winds will turn \nwesterly Tuesday morning as a trough of low pressure passes \nacross the area, but the expected main cold front with \nnortherly winds may be delayed until Tuesday afternoon/evening. \nThis would allow for another potentially very warm day Tuesday \nwith the blended temps ranging from the upper 70s north to the \nmid 80s south of I-80 and these are certainly possible, so made \nno changes from this distance.\n\nAs for precip chances, there is a small chance for some warm air\nadvection showers Monday morning that appear they will remain \nnorth of the area. Then as the above mentioned trough moves \nacross the area Tuesday morning, there could be some showers and\npossibly a few thunderstorms, but coverage looks rather low and\nexpecting much of the area to remain dry.\n\nA better chance for showers and thunderstorms, for at least part\nof the area will arrive Tuesday night into Wednesday morning as\nthe cold front is pushing south into central IL and central IN.\nCurrent trends suggest the best chances for rain will be south\nof I-80, though still uncertainty for the northern extent with\nthis precip potential. High pressure will quickly move across\nthe northern Great Lakes Wednesday night, which should push the\nfront and precip chances far enough south for a break in the\nprecip until the front lifts back north Thursday ahead of a\nstronger cold front that arrives Thursday night or Friday\nmorning. Despite very good ensemble support for at least a few\ndays of much colder temperatures across the region, the ECMWF is\nwarmer and adds uncertainty to the overall temp expectations. \nBlended guidance now has low temps in the mid/upper 30s for next\nSunday morning and these seem reasonable given the ensembles. \nThough if the cold air does materialize across the region, temps\ncould be even colder. cms",
